  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A malaria free Vanuatu, contributing to the good health and well-being of the population - Oceania - Multicountry Western Pacific (01-Jan-2021-31-Dec-2023)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Multicountry Western Pacific, Malaria, United Nations Development Programme (UNDP Pacific Office in Fiji)This program has the following goals: Prevent re-establishment of transmission in all provinces where transmission has been interrupted.  Achieve zero indigenous malaria cases in all provinces of Vanuatu by the end of 2023.  Receive World Health Organization (WHO) certification of malaria-free status in 2026. The objectives of this program are: To maintain universal coverage with long-lasting insecticidal mosquito nets (LLIN); and to rapidly reduce malaria transmission in selected high burden areas using indoor residual spraying (IRS)..  To roll out case-based surveillance and response nationwide using the &#x2018;1-7-60&#x2019; approach.  To test all fever cases for malaria by rapid diagnostic test (RDT) or microscopy, and provide prompt radical treatment and care for all confirmed cases according to the national Malaria Diagnosis and Treatment Guidelines..  To mobilize communities through health promotion and leverage the support of all stakeholders in a multi-sectoral effort to accelerate the elimination of malaria..  To ensure that malaria and other VBD prevention, surveillance and case management are well integrated into disaster preparedness and response activities.  Supporting Environment Objective: To maintain a high level of political commitment to malaria elimination; and to strengthen program management at national level and implementation at provincial and local levels through improved mechanisms for workforce management, program planning, disbursement of funds, information and data management, technical assistance and cooperation, procurement and supply chain management, and performance monitoring..  Supporting Environment Objective:  To leverage technical partnerships in support of innovation by generating new knowledge and applying it to improve delivery and quality of malaria services.</narrative>
  </description>
